WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:03.940
Movie sets must be the perfect workplaces
for screw-ups and joking around.

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:07.220
As gag reels have shown us, it doesn't matter
what kind of movie the actors are working

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:07.720
on.

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:11.720
Whether it's a low-rent comedy or an Oscar-winning
epic, sometimes the actors just can't keep

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:14.860
a straight face when they're supposed to be
taking things much more seriously.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:16.360
Chop, chop

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:20.860
Liam Neeson definitely has "a special set
of skills," but in real life, it's not being

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:23.310
good at killing people — it's acting well.

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:27.230
In films like Schindler's List and Rob Roy,
Neeson showed that he's a serious actor who

00:00:27.230 --> 00:00:29.240
doesn't meet his roles halfway.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:32.699
Except on the set of The A-Team, where it's
pretty clear that Neeson's "special set of

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:36.000
skills" doesn't include being able to say
the words "attack choppers."

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.060
"There's two attack chopters…

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:40.340
"Hahahaha."

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:43.340
"Pike buys out every seat on the train.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:45.480
There's two attack choppers..

00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:46.580
F--- it."

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:48.460
A crowded elevator

00:00:48.789 --> 00:00:53.240
Oscar-winner Philip Seymour Hoffman, Oscar
nominees Joaquin Phoenix and Amy Adams, and

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.370
Emmy-winner Rami Malek step into an elevator.

00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:58.859
It sounds like the setup to a joke, but it's
just another day on the set of Paul Thomas

00:00:58.859 --> 00:01:02.539
Anderson's The Master — a serious movie
about serious things.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:03.220
And then...

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.700
"(Fart sound)"

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.320
It's the one thing you're never supposed to
do, especially around Oscar winners.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.780
But to everyone's credit, they found it pretty
funny.

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:16.880
Well, everyone except Joaquin Phoenix.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.520
Joaquin Phoenix does not laugh if the script
does not tell Joaquin Phoenix to laugh.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:23.240
Burning sensation

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.720
Harvard-educated, Oscar-winning actress Natalie
Portman took on a rare comedic role in the

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:31.670
2010 spoof Your Highness , a movie with some
pretty ridiculous moments.

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:32.760
"I don't want to think about that."

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:38.640
"Let's just say that we were moments late
and he was able to… get her cookies."

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.020
"Shut up."

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:44.250
Portman gave the film's silly dialogue her
all, but there's one line she had a really

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:45.420
hard time getting through.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:47.480
"I know the feeling well.

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:48.860
It's been burning in my beaver since… (hahah)"

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:52.400
"It's been burning in my bh-ah..."

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:53.720
At least she tried!

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:55.520
There will be bloopers

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.560
Daniel Day-Lewis has won three Oscars for
Best Actor in a Leading Role, which is more

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:01.100
than anyone else in history.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:05.030
He obviously puts a lot of work into his performances,
to the point of staying in character even

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:06.410
when the cameras aren't rolling.

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:10.790
Day-Lewis doesn't even break character between
takes…and yet here he is, during There Will

00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:14.109
Be Blood, cracking up with the actor who plays
his son after a take.

00:02:14.109 --> 00:02:15.220
He's human after all.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:16.860
Pug power play

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:22.060
1997's sci-fi comedy Men In Black sees odd
couple special agents played by Tommy Lee

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:25.600
Jones and Will Smith take on a whole galaxy
of alien oddities.

00:02:25.849 --> 00:02:28.379
One of their toughest foes comes in the form
of a pug.

00:02:28.379 --> 00:02:32.310
To get information out of it, Jones' Agent
K has to shake the crap out of it.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:37.490
The thing is, that was a real dog, so Tommy
Lee Jones had to actually shake it and actually

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:40.320
demand information...from a little dog in
his hands.

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:43.060
"Rosenberg said something about a galaxy on
Orion's belt what's he talking about Frank?"

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:43.860
"Beats me?"

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:44.540
"Beats you?"

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:48.800
"Rolf rolf oh no oh oh"

00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:52.800
It must have been a ridiculous scene to film,
because even in the final cut, you can see

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:55.640
Tommy Lee Jones start to smile once before
the camera cuts.

00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:57.180
Hand in glove

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:02.540
One of the funniest moments in Kubrick's 1964
Cold War satire Dr. Strangelove or: How I

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:06.930
Learned to Stop Worrying and Love the Bomb
finds the former Nazi scientist Dr. Strangelove—played

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:10.420
by the incomparable Peter Sellers—struggling
to control his own arm.

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:15.800
"Combined with a spirit of bored curiosity
for the adventure ahead..

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:16.420
Ha!"

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:23.390
Although Sellers never breaks character, Peter
Bull, standing to his left in the shot, can't

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:25.640
quite keep it together during the off-the-wall
performance.

00:03:31.340 --> 00:03:32.960
And, really, can you blame him?

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:34.380
Acting suspect

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:39.099
The infamous lineup sequence in The Usual
Suspects was supposed to be a lot more serious.

00:03:39.099 --> 00:03:41.489
Unfortunately, the cast wouldn't play ball.

00:03:41.489 --> 00:03:44.219
According to Kevin Pollak, they just couldn't
take the line seriously.

00:03:44.219 --> 00:03:48.029
And according to Gabriel Byrne, Benicio Del
Toro would not stop farting.

00:03:48.029 --> 00:03:52.530
Eventually, director Bryan Singer gave up,
took a few different takes, and spliced them

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:53.530
together.

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:54.340
And a classic scene was born.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.480
(laughter)

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:03.120
Thanks for watching!

